<aside .import-note>
**Imported from JATS — a live interoperability demonstration.** The article below is a real, published journal paper, rendered by Enscribe. It was produced automatically from JATS XML by the `enscribe import-jats` command, with no manual editing, to show that Enscribe interoperates with the existing academic-publishing ecosystem.

**Source.** Sullivan AD, Wigginton J, Kirschner D. *The coreceptor mutation CCR5Δ32 influences the dynamics of HIV epidemics and is selected for by HIV.* *Proc. Natl. Acad. Sci. USA* **98**(18):10214–10219 (2001). DOI <a https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.181325198 | 10.1073/pnas.181325198>. Copyright © 2001, The National Academy of Sciences. Reproduced from the <a https://jats.nlm.nih.gov/publishing/tag-library/1.3/chapter/samples.html | NLM JATS Tag Library> sample-article set, used here to demonstrate JATS import.

**What was simplified during import.** Four tables use multi-column header layouts that Enscribe's native table syntax cannot express, so their grid is preserved as an HTML layout — but every cell is converted natively like the rest of the document: the in-cell formulas render, and the in-cell citations, cross-references, and footnotes resolve (the tables are also numbered, as before). The figure images are not distributed with the article XML, so the figures show their captions only. Two of the article's nine equations cannot be rendered: their source LaTeX (the `<tex-math>` carried in the JATS) contains malformed `\left`/`\right` — a `\left` with no delimiter argument — that KaTeX cannot parse, so they appear as small error markers. These are defects in the publisher's source, passed through faithfully rather than silently rewritten; they are not artifacts of the import. Everything else is imported and rendered natively: the abstract, the section structure, the remaining mathematics, the citations and bibliography, and the cross-references to figures, tables, and equations.
</aside>
